WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:03.129
This is Timor-Leste.

00:00:03.129 --> 00:00:09.559
If you, dear viewer, were born before May
20th, 2002, then congratulations, you are

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:15.730
older than this country — the fifth-youngest
internationally-recognized sovereign nation.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:21.590
At 19 years old, it would qualify as a legal
adult in some parts of the world.

00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:27.700
Timor-Leste, meaning “East East” in Malay
and Portuguese — is situated in south southeast

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.950
Asia — just above Australia.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:36.750
Its closest neighbor, however, is Indonesia,
with whom it shares half the island of Timor

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.410
— about the size of Hawai’i.

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:43.720
But, just to complicate things, part of its
territory is governed as a detached Special

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:47.650
Administrative Region inside Indonesia.

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:52.130
Like many young, developing nations, Timor-Leste
is poor.

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:53.820
Really poor.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.630
Approximately 40% of the population is unable
to afford daily necessities and over half

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:04.100
of all children under the age of five are
developmentally stunted — more than all

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:06.759
but 2 other countries.

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:13.079
The problem is essentially this: To live longer,
happier lives, the country needs more and

00:01:13.079 --> 00:01:14.560
better jobs.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:21.439
Currently, only about 25% of the working-age
population is employed in the formal economy.

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:27.990
The rest either can’t find work or are preoccupied
with farming or basic household duties.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.840
Now, to get jobs, it needs some form of investment.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:37.039
And to get investment, it would need some
kind of resource worthy of extraction.

00:01:37.039 --> 00:01:40.759
And, in fact, it does.

00:01:40.759 --> 00:01:45.029
As of 2020, Timor-Leste’s GDP was $1.8 billion.

00:01:45.029 --> 00:01:52.149
And yet just 90-miles off its coast sits somewhere
between twenty and fifty billion dollars — depending

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:56.709
on prices — worth of oil and liquid petroleum
gas.

00:01:56.709 --> 00:01:58.850
This is not a new discovery.

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:02.639
Its existence has been known to the world
for nearly fifty years.

00:02:02.639 --> 00:02:06.819
There’s just one tiny problem...

00:02:06.819 --> 00:02:12.450
Sponsored by CuriosityStream and Nebula — where
you can watch the bonus video which accompanies

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:16.140
this one for just $15 bucks a year.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.260
Timor-Leste’s path to independence was…
tumultuous, to say the least.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:28.310
In 1998, after twenty years of violent Indonesian
occupation in which hundreds of thousands

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:34.269
were killed, pro-democracy protests led to
the resignation of its president, Suharto.

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:40.290
His replacement was more amenable to change,
or, at least its appearance, and in 1999,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.600
called a referendum on independence.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:49.379
Between becoming a special, autonomous region
of Indonesia, and forming a new, independent

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:56.819
country, an overwhelming majority of the 450,000
voters — 78% — chose the latter.

00:02:56.819 --> 00:03:01.579
Not expecting this outcome and having only
wished to use the vote to win legitimacy,

00:03:01.579 --> 00:03:06.030
Indonesia began a new campaign of total destruction.

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:11.250
During this time of UN-maintained limbo, nearly
all of the region’s infrastructure was burned

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:17.019
to the ground and its people forcibly shipped
to the western, Indonesian side.

00:03:17.019 --> 00:03:23.930
By the time of formal independence in 2002,
Timor-Leste had, at last, a strong, healthy

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:27.940
democracy, and… not much else.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:31.129
Its newfound freedom had truly cost it everything.

00:03:31.129 --> 00:03:35.599
There was, perhaps, only one silver lining.

00:03:35.599 --> 00:03:40.110
It had one thing few other young nations do:
oil.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.459
Lots of it.

00:03:41.459 --> 00:03:44.480
And the timing could not have been better.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.549
This is a graph of global oil prices.

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:54.370
Its first and only developed oil field, Bayu
Undan, was built here, in 2004, when construction

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.000
costs were at their lowest.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.540
And this is when Bayu Undan reached peak production,
around 70 million barrels a year — precisely

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.260
when oil prices reached their highest ever
on record.

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:12.190
Because of this, fragile, young Tim or-Leste’s
sovereign wealth fund was the only one to

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:16.360
survive the 2008 financial crisis unscathed.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:21.110
It was an amazing stroke of luck for the place
that needed it the most.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:24.570
But no oil field lasts forever.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:32.220
Oil revenue peaked in 2012 at $3.56 billion
and has been in sharp decline since.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:36.640
Now this would be concerning — disastrous,
even — for any country.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:40.830
But for Timor-Leste, this could be existential.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:48.220
As the 2nd most oil-dependent country on Earth,
90% of its public revenue and 80% of its GDP

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:51.190
comes from this one resource.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:55.540
None of this money, however, has translated
into long-term development.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.280
Its economy has not fundamentally changed
despite these riches.

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:06.790
Between 2014 and 18, the number of private
employees fell by 15%, despite the working-age

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:10.000
population increasing by 10%.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:14.260
Instead, oil has only delayed the inevitable.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:17.760
Meanwhile, the money is rapidly running out.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.790
This is the amount of oil money the government
could spend sustainably — that is, only

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.010
the interest.

00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:27.090
And this is how much it is spending.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:33.210
If trends continue, experts predict the money
will be all gone in 10-15 years.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:39.210
Which brings us to the Greater Sunrise Field
— five trillion cubic feet of gas and 225

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:45.310
million barrels of condensate worth $50 billion
southeast of Timor-Leste.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:51.690
Although located in deeper water, Greater
Sunrise is actually closer than Bayu-Undan.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:57.810
And this time the country has a plan to convert
this temporary windfall into lasting prosperity.

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:03.060
The difference is that this time the oil will
be processed onshore, creating local jobs

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:06.750
and, in theory, developing the wider economy.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:11.460
The “Tasi Mane” project, as it’s known,
involves converting over a thousand hectares

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:18.000
of agricultural land across three towns into
interconnected, petroleum processing facilities.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.490
If managed well, this could be step one on
the path to Timor-Leste’s development — a

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:27.120
pivotal turning point for the entire country.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.410
So what, exactly, is the problem?

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.970
It all started with oysters.

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:39.240
As the world began figuring out who owned
which parts of the ocean, Australia’s primary

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:45.150
concern was preventing Japanese fishermen
from harvesting oysters off its Northern coast.

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:51.810
The big debate, at the time, was: Should maritime
boundaries be drawn halfway between two countries,

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.940
or following the natural geology of continental
shelves?

00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:01.510
For Australia, with a large continental shelf
and a dozen tiny neighbors, the answer was

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:02.920
obvious.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:08.890
And so, after U.S. President Harry Truman
set the precedent by taking the latter approach,

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:12.840
Australia saw its golden opportunity to do
likewise.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:17.890
But it would soon find something far more
profitable than oysters.

00:07:17.890 --> 00:07:23.650
Having unilaterally claimed ownership of this
entire region — which came as close as 30

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:28.290
miles to the Timor coast — it began confidently
searching for oil.

00:07:28.290 --> 00:07:35.330
By 1965, Australia had issued exploration
permits to Woodside, Shell, ARCO, and several

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:41.230
others for areas far closer to Indonesia than
its own land borders.

00:07:41.230 --> 00:07:46.180
The two countries then reached a border agreement
that heavily favored Australia.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.150
But that still left a giant question mark
over this area.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:57.230
East Timor was then a Portuguese colony, whom
Australia refused to negotiate with.

00:07:57.230 --> 00:07:59.460
Instead, it waited.

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:06.370
And, in 1975, Indonesia solved this problem
for Australia by invading Portuguese Timor.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:11.530
It sat by as up to 200,000 people were killed.

00:08:11.530 --> 00:08:16.920
In 1989, they reached an agreement that split
the gap into three zones — one in which

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:23.100
oil revenue would be shared equally, and two
in which one country was entitled to 90%.

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:28.810
Minus the protests at home, it went exactly
as Australia had hoped.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:34.610
These boundaries even remained after Timor-Leste
became independent 13 years later, except

00:08:34.610 --> 00:08:39.540
that the new country was now entitled to 90%
of revenue from the shared zone, rather than

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:41.330
just half.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:47.220
Its people had no say during Portuguese colonial
times, were represented by Indonesian occupiers

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:54.330
for the next 25 years, and even after independence,
only inherited the previous agreement.

00:08:54.330 --> 00:09:00.070
But it still had not yet seen the full-lengths
Australia would go to steal its oil.

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:05.390
In 2006, the two governments signed a new
agreement that essentially postponed the topic

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:09.400
of maritime boundaries in the interest of
just getting drilling.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:14.150
The profits from the Greater Sunrise field,
the crown jewel, remember, would now be split

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:15.340
50/50.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:20.430
6 years later, the world learned some shocking
news.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:24.850
During the negotiations, the Australian Secret
Service had planted hidden listening devices

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:28.650
near the office of Timor-Leste’s Prime Minister.

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:33.180
Even after word got out, it refused to invalidate
the treaty.

00:09:33.180 --> 00:09:38.370
Only in 2018, after bringing the case to the
International Court of Justice, did they finally

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:40.470
resolve the dispute.

00:09:40.470 --> 00:09:45.390
Under the new agreement, Timor-Leste will
get 70% of the revenue if the oil is piped

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:50.640
to its shores, and 80% if it flows through
Australia.

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.620
In 2019, the country spent $650 million in
public funds to buy a controlling stake in

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:58.530
the oil field.

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:05.730
After nearly half a century of waiting, Timor-Leste
finally regained control of its own destiny.

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:08.770
But it may have been too late.

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:14.600
Australia’s negotiating strategy, apart
from outright bullying, was always to delay.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:19.640
Unlike its neighbor, it didn’t need the
money urgently and knew time was on its side.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:25.710
Now, today, with fossil fuels slowly on the
way out and as oil prices plummeted during

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:29.450
the pandemic, Timor-Leste finds itself in
a dilemma:

00:10:29.450 --> 00:10:34.530
On one hand, it could learn the lesson of
other oil-dependent nations and process the

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:40.730
oil and gas onshore — creating new jobs
and hopefully growing the overall economy.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:46.970
But independent analysts say, at 70%, accounting
for the cost to build the infrastructure,

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:49.300
it would only break even.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:52.040
After all that work, it wouldn’t even make
money.

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:58.380
In fact, the project is so economically questionable
that even China — famous for its “debt-trap

00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:01.440
diplomacy” — refused to fund it.

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:06.420
On the other hand, it could instead pipe the
oil to Australia, in which case it would receive

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.529
80% of the revenue.

00:11:08.529 --> 00:11:12.580
But in that case, it would eventually return
right back where it started — with no new

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:15.470
jobs or economic activity.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:20.050
The Tasi-Mane project has been ridiculed as
a “White Elephant”.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:24.950
Its four-lane “superhighway” literally
crumbling apart and its pristine yet empty

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:30.110
airport have all the hallmarks of irresponsible
populace-pleasing spending.

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:36.420
Still, the question remains: What should it
do to escape its state of poverty?

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:41.990
The unfortunate truth is that: there may not
be a good answer.

00:11:41.990 --> 00:11:47.910
The reason for Timor Leste’s troubles ultimately
comes down to maritime border ambiguities.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:52.520
Throughout history, the sea has been divided
by doing everything from drawing a line right

00:11:52.520 --> 00:11:57.030
down the middle, to measuring the distance
a cannon could shoot from shore.

00:11:57.030 --> 00:12:02.730
We explore this question in more depth in
this exclusive bonus video on Nebula called

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:05.030
“Who owns the ocean?”.

